+  // [ps5 port] Volatile pages (the shared_memory_volatile_pages setting).
+  //
+  // A page the game writes after it was uploaded is caught by write-protecting
+  // it: the write faults, the page is unprotected and marked not valid, and the
+  // next draw that needs it uploads and protects it again. A page of geometry
+  // the game rewrites every frame goes round that loop every frame, and on the
+  // PS5 a protection change is slow (about 0.1 ms, with the global lock held):
+  // the profile of a console had the command processor in mprotect for a third
+  // of its time and the game's main thread for an eighth.
+  //
+  // So a page that keeps faulting stops being protected. Whether it changed is
+  // found out when a draw asks for it instead, from a 64-bit hash of it kept
+  // from the last upload (16 KB: under a microsecond; the first version kept a
+  // copy and compared, which read twice the memory and took two). What a draw
+  // gets is the same either way: what is in the game's memory when the draw is
+  // made.
+  //
+  // A look costs every time the page is asked for, where a protected page
+  // costs nothing until it is written. So a page asked for many times in a row
+  // without having changed is protected again; having been volatile once, its
+  // next write fault makes it volatile at once. The worst case is then what it
+  // was before, plus those looks. How many: a fault and the protecting that
+  // follows cost about as much as 130 looks, and giving up at the break-even
+  // is the most that can be had without knowing what comes (at 16, a console
+  // had 33 pages a frame going round).
+  //
+  // Only pages nothing else relies on being told about: one a texture watches,
+  // or one the GPU wrote, is protected as before.
+  static constexpr uint8_t kVolatileFaultCount = 3;      // write faults, each within...
+  static constexpr uint64_t kVolatileFaultGapMs = 2000;  // ...this long of the one before
+  static constexpr uint32_t kVolatileQuietCompares = 128; // unchanged in a row: protected again
+  static constexpr uint32_t kVolatileMaxPages = 16384;
+  struct VolatilePage {
+    // In the global critical region:
+    uint64_t fault_ms = 0;  // the last write fault
+    uint8_t faults = 0;     // write faults in a row
+    bool sticky = false;    // has been volatile: the next fault makes it so again
+    // Goes up whenever the page becomes volatile or stops being: a hash kept
+    // under another number is of no use. (Written in the global critical
+    // region, read by the command processor thread outside it.)
